# rm360, the bark king (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm360
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier standard
written_for rm_eleanor_wickhand
written_by codex
title the bark king
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=woodcutter
traits temper=even talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=devout drive=ambitious
quirk leaves_bark_on_one_draught_piece_to_mark_a_king
likes smoked_eel draughts market_bustle misty_dawns
dislikes haggling wet_floors
fears a_warped_playing_square an_empty_evening_pot
hopes a_board_worthy_of_a_long_game a_clear_sight_of_the_far_row
topics pastimes +0.5 work +0.4 faith +0.3
styles comfort=practical gossip=plain refuse=firm tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# keeps the bark on one draught piece, strives to see two moves past a crowded middle, and speaks with no patience for a price that changes halfway through a purchase.

words
oath! = King in the sawdust | Knot across the diagonal
saying! = The farthest square grows bark | Look beyond the jumped square
roughking = my bark-backed king | my rough-crowned piece
softside = the peeled pale face | the smooth side
farrow = the king row | the last bright row
gamebox = my chip box | the sliding piece box
jointline = the join between the boards | a pale seam
eeledge = a smoky tail cut | a strip of rich eel
miststrip = a pale veil over a board | an edge of mist
strawcloth = a rough clearing rag | a square of wool
woodknock = the crack of dry ends | a short wooden knock
knoteye = a dark seam in the cut | a tight eye in the grain

any
opinion @1 church for 2 : Faith has let me look beyond a bad position and see a neighbour waiting for a hand. That matters more than winning any little game.
opinion @1 dole for 3 : Giving out bread has one rule plain enough to put on a board. Get a meal into a hungry mouth before anybody goes home.
opinion @1 houses against 3 : A House seems born sitting on the far row. The rest of us would like a clear square even to begin.
opinion @1 merchants against 2 : I'd sooner one hard price on a scrap of board than a kind word that grows dearer while I'm counting.
opinion @1 drink against 1 : I'd like to choose which move is mine. Too much ale can take the choice and leave a wolf the entire blame.
opinion @1 sump for 1 : A Sump door can open on two seats and a game and give me all the room I need. The hill's fine games can't do more.

grown
line @1 greet : I've set the pieces aside for a caller.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : A caller I haven't placed yet, in the nearest row. Welcome.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : There's light to see the pale pieces now. Come near.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : We can leave the board dark and talk.
line @5 greet [self.working=yes] : I've a cut to finish neatly before I turn.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Come sit by the edge we know between us.
line @1 again.open : The far row's no farther off than when we parted.
line @1 greet.return : You have an answer across a plain stretch of board.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : A friendly word's got through the centre cleanly.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : Your greeting carried beyond the wood knocking.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Your voice shows up better than the squares do now.
line @1 farewell : May the street open up once you've crossed it.
line @1 farewell.return : My turn to watch the loose pieces. Go well.
line @1 react.news : Put the moving part before me and leave it there a moment.
line @1 react.news.new : I hadn't reckoned on that piece moving.
line @1 react.news.known : I'd seen the position before, if not quite from there.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Did the teller see the piece or just its empty square?
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : May that house find somebody who'll carry a little of the ordinary weight.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Fair room for a good move there.
line @1 answer.how : I've a firm square under me and another in view.
line @1 ask.how : Seen a way clear through today's business yet?
line @1 sympathise : Tough to see past an empty place meant for somebody else.
line @1 reassure : Wait for a patch of clear ground. There's no clock on grief.
line @1 decline : I'd have to call a crooked bargain straight, and I won't.
line @1 accept_decline : I'll keep my hand clear of your pieces.
line @1 thank : You let me see another move when I'd seen none.
line @1 deflect_thanks : Give the next learner a decent turn.
line @1 preclose : My board has slipped half a square across my knee.
line @1 accept_preclose : I can carry on without rushing you.
line @1 ack : Sound square.
scene barkking
when topic=pastimes self.likes=draughts
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I leave a rim of bark on the piece that earns the far row.
b : For a crown? | Doesn't the bark catch?
a : Yes. Even if the light's gone I can feel which piece earned its wider choice. | Only if I push it sideways. I expect better manners of a king.

scene markboard
when topic=work self.job=woodcutter self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I turn a scrap sideways to see where the grain means to run.
b : What do you see across it? | Can you choose another way?
a : Little black eyes in the pale end. Some mean there's a knot ahead of the cut. | My hands can. The board will give its own opinion after the cut.

scene eel
when topic=food self.likes=smoked_eel
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A smoky eel end keeps its taste longer if I set my bite across the grain.
b : Chewing slowly? | You find grain in fish?
a : Quite slowly when there's only a small supper. A careful mouth has its compensations. | Everything put together has a direction it comes apart. Supper is a convenient lesson.

scene market
when topic=pastimes self.likes=market_bustle
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I stand quietly in a busy market to see which bit of the crowd moves next.
b : Is that a sort of game? | Do you ever get pushed about?
a : Until I have to pick a way through. Then it's quite practical. | Yes, if I'm so pleased with a guess that I forget my place in it.

scene mist
when topic=weather self.likes=misty_dawns
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : On a misty morning a single clear stone can make a whole road visible in my mind.
b : Would you walk on that guess? | Why remember the stone?
a : Just one stone's worth of walking. A pleasant vision needn't make a poor foothold. | It usually shows the edge of a place where the water gathers. I'd rather let the water keep that square.

scene wetboard
when topic=weather self.dislikes=wet_floors
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I keep my pieces in their box when a floor's wet.
b : Do you play on the floor? | Would a table do?
a : Sometimes. More often I'm afraid of losing my footing while carrying a board full of draughts. | If the table's near enough to reach walking slowly. One splash can finish a game without choosing a winner.

scene singleprice
when topic=prices self.dislikes=haggling
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I want the same price when the coin leaves my pocket as when my paw went in.
b : Won't you argue a price down? | Even for the last little piece?
a : Tell me the real figure and I'll decide. I don't want to perform my poverty as part of a sale. | If a small piece costs less, name it before I choose it. My ears are free before my purse opens.

scene stairstep
when topic=land self.quarter=sump
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I think of the steep Sump stairs as a diagonal laid upright.
b : Can you jump two? | Where's your king row?
a : Dry stairs, clear feet, sometimes. I don't get an extra life for calling a lane a board. | Where the pot and my chair begin. No better reward for a hill.

scene foodprayer
when topic=faith self.piety=devout
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I like a prayer that sends me away with my sleeves rolled back for a neighbour.
b : Doesn't quiet suit a prayer? | What if the work's finished already?
a : Quiet to listen and activity to put what I've heard to use. The two fit well together. | A heartfelt thanks then. Perhaps the neighbour would welcome company at supper.

scene blankpieces
when topic=pastimes self.likes=draughts
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I give a beginner a pair of plain pieces first and a rough king to feel afterwards.
b : Why hold the king back? | Why just a pair?
a : To make the change of direction clear when it comes. A crown means something once you've been one way. | To show a single jump. A whole crowded board can wait till there's a reason for the first move.

scene dearwood
when topic=prices self.wealth=poor|destitute
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Good offcuts are dear in time even when they're free in money.
b : What costs the time? | Would you buy a board ready-made?
a : Finding one that's flat under all four corners. I won't waste a whole working evening chasing a draught off a tilt. | If that price bought me back the evening for playing, perhaps. Most would cost the next evening too.

scene friend
when topic=pastimes self.friend
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : There's a little plain piece I'd give #self.friend# to keep.
b : What good's one draught on its own? | Why that piece?
a : It'll bring a game to mind, and the full box can come along next time. | It's got a dark eye on the face. Whatever else gets lost in the chips, I'd recognise that one.

scene ambition
when topic=dreams
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I dream of a board the width of a whole long table, each square true against the next.
b : Who gets the first game? | How would the far player reach?
a : I'd like a beginner. I'd rather see somebody delighted to arrive than bored with an easy victory. | That's the detail I keep waking before I answer. Dreams can afford long arms.

scene slipstack
when topic=work self.job=woodcutter
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I set dry lengths so air can pass behind the pile.
b : Why keep air in room you could fill with wood? | Is that to make the board dry faster?
a : Because spoiled wood takes just as much room and gives no useful work back. | To let both faces dry evenly. One dry face and one damp one are an invitation to a twist.

scene muddledvoice
when topic=gossip
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I don't give a name to a shadow on a board.
b : What if the outline is familiar? | What would convince you?
a : A round-headed nail casts a very fair draught shadow. I need more before I tell a wolf's business. | The piece itself and a reason to speak. I've no call to move somebody else's king.

scene silentmeal
when topic=faith
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I let my eyes take in the whole table before I start to eat.
b : Do you count the dishes? | Is it a custom?
a : I look for the wolf nobody has passed a dish to. An empty plate can sit quite politely and go unseen. | My own. I like to know if there's a paw somewhere a little hesitant to reach.

scene endday
when topic=day here.time=dusk|night
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I rub the rough king once before I put the game away by night.
b : To knock the dust out? | To remember the winner?
a : To feel whether a flake of bark is coming loose. A crown too can do with repairs. | A board can start again tomorrow without keeping a grievance from the previous game.

scene newmove
when topic=smalltalk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A move that looked wonderfully bold from this end can look foolish from the other.
b : Would you change seats halfway? | Who gets to decide which view was right?
a : Before a game, yes. While it's on I'll live with what I opened. | The next move usually does. A quiet answer from the other side is quite educational.

story fallenking [topic=memories]
  : Once I played with bark so loose that the king's rim came away halfway over the board.
  : Nobody could feel which of two pale pieces was meant to go backwards.
  b : Did you start all over?
  : We looked at the scrap of bark. Its pale underside fitted one piece exactly. I found my king and a firmer crown for the next evening.

story squarepot [topic=food self.likes=smoked_eel]
  : I spread my smoky eel across a pair of squares to see where to cut an even share.
  : I pulled it tight to measure. When I let go both ends curled and one share looked enormous.
  b : How did you settle that?
  : By the weight in my palm. The board was very good at its own game and a fool at supper.

story dustdawn [topic=work self.job=woodcutter]
  : A cool mist had left a dark map of marks on a stack of boards one morning.
  : I touched the pale shape at its centre and lifted one thin shaving off the end.
  b : Was the shaving keeping the wet out?
  : It was. A thing almost too small to pick up had drawn quite a clear picture of itself.

rule @cut
when concept=bark self.job=woodcutter self.working=yes
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Let the new cut show its own grain. | Set the short ends where they won't tip.

rule @king
when concept=bark self.likes=draughts
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Feel the rough edge before you take the king. | One clear diagonal, please.

rule @wet
when concept=bark here.weather=rain
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Board level through the wet patch. | Keep the game box shut in the drip.

rule @market
when concept=bark here.place=market
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Name the figure while the hands are free. | One square of space to pass.

dlg @1 resident.hub : I'll clear a square for whatever thought you've brought.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : There's the familiar piece again, #topic.player#.
dlg @3 resident.hub [pair.disposition=wary|hostile] : Keep your paws to the near squares. We're within easy speaking distance.
dlg @4 resident.hub [here.time=night] : The last row's nearly invisible now but I can hear a voice well enough.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=woodcutter] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. I like a cut that runs true all the way into the useful wood.
dlg @2 resident.job : I take a look at both faces before putting a piece to use.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : The news fits on this bit of the board. #world.news#.
dlg @1 resident.bye : Save one clear move for coming home.
graft @barkpiece resident.hub
choice How did this piece earn its bark? -> barkpiece
node barkpiece
say The bark was always there. I left it on one piece and peeled the rest. A wolf who gets a plain draught to the far row can trade it in for this one.
choice Does the bark really change the move? -> barkpiece_more
choice Hand the draught back. -> hub
node barkpiece_more
say The rules do. The bark lets a paw read the rule when the lamp's a long way off. Try the plain piece and the rough one with your eyes shut.
choice Hand the draught back. -> hub
choice Leave the king on its square. -> bye
